  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1155 PM CDT Mon Oct 26 2026\n\nPrecip and Storm Chances...Rain showers and sprinkles will\ncontinue overnight into early Tuesday, mainly along and north of\nHwy 29, as a shortwave, weak low pressure and push of WAA move \nacross MN and western WI. Highest PoPs (90%) will be over northern\nWI, tapering off as you move south. Models have backed off on the\nsub-freezing layer aloft, along with surface temps expected to \nstay above freezing (and a warm ground), so just a cold rain is \nexpected across far northern WI tonight into early Tuesday. Mainly\ndry conditions are expected on Tuesday, but a few light showers \nor sprinkles will remain possible within the moist, WAA regime. \nAnother shortwave and frontal boundary will cross the region late\nWednesday into Thursday morning, bringing a round of showers \n(70-100% chance). A ribbon of mainly elevated instability will \nmove into the area late Wednesday (likely after sunset), bringing \nlow thunder chances (15-30%, highest south). No severe weather is\nexpected. Rainfall totals from Tuesday morning through Thursday \nmorning look to remain on the light side for most/all of the area,\nwith probabilities of over 0.25\" near 50% from Wood to Vilas \nCounty, but probabilities for over 0.50\" under 25%. But any rain \nwill help with the ongoing drought across northern WI. High \npressure settles over the Great Lakes on Thursday, with additional\nshower chances arriving this weekend.\n\nTemperatures...after a brief return to more seasonal temps on \nMonday, a return to above normal temps starts on Tuesday with \nhighs rebounding back into the mid 50s to mid 60s. On Wednesday, \nstrong southerly flow in the warm sector will allow temps to climb\ninto the upper 60s to mid 70s, away from the shore of Lake \nMichigan. This will be in record territory for at few sites (Green\nBay, Marshfield and Wausau) with a few other sites nearing their \nrecords. Temps drop a little on Thursday with highs mainly in the\n60s, then the above normal temps continue through the weekend, \nwith highs in the in the mid 50s to mid 60s.",
